From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: McKernel Rust Port <mckernel-rust@localhost>
Date: Mon, 17 Aug 2026 17:35:00 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] rust: update the no-allocator-shim marker for Rust 1.92

Rust 1.92's `alloc` crate calls a function named
`__rust_no_alloc_shim_is_unstable_v2` before the global allocation entry
points.  Rust upstream changed this compatibility marker from an unmangled
static to an internally mangled Rust function in rust-lang/rust#141061.

Linux 6.12 still provides the old static marker because its allocator glue
predates that Rust change.  Define the exact function form tested by Rust
upstream and enable the internal compiler attribute in the kernel crate.  The
function is deliberately compiled into `rust/kernel.o`; it is not a linker
stub supplied by the validation workflow.

Rust 1.92 warns that `rustc_attrs` is an internal feature.  The exact build
promotes warnings to errors, so narrowly allow `internal_features` immediately
next to this one required crate feature rather than weakening build warnings.

Current upstream Linux removed the standard `alloc` crate and `GlobalAlloc`
in 392e34b6bc22077ef63abf62387ea3e9f39418c1.  That larger allocator rewrite
is not suitable for this frozen Linux 6.12 source, so this is a local
compatibility adaptation rather than a backport of that commit.

